  "response": "Sociologists use the term concentrated disadvantage to describe neighborhoods with overlapping poverty, joblessness, and family disruption. Whether such contexts shape life chances, or merely assemble people whose trajectories were already fixed, has structured the field's methodological quarrels for a generation. Christopher Jencks and Susan Mayer's 1990 review argued that existing neighborhood studies could not separate contextual influence from residential sorting, and Robert Sampson's book Great American City documented the persistence of neighborhood inequality in Chicago across several decades; a body of work incapable of distinguishing context from sorting cannot be squared with a documented multi-decade record of neighborhood inequality in one city.\n\nThat unresolved impasse over separating context from sorting led directly to the adoption of within-family comparison as a remedy. Sibling-difference designs hold constant characteristics shared by children of the same parents. The selection problem arises when unmeasured characteristics of residents determine which neighborhoods they live in.\n\nIt was precisely because the determinants of residential location remain unmeasured that the discipline turned toward designs resting on chance assignment. Unlike strategies that hold fixed whatever children of the same parents have in common, this newer approach seeks its leverage in variation imposed from outside the household. Researchers use housing voucher lotteries as instrumental variables to identify neighborhood effects, a practice brought about by randomized housing mobility experiments, which are designed to break the link between family characteristics and neighborhood context. That ambition to sever family background from neighborhood context resulted in the Moving to Opportunity demonstration, launched by the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development in 1994.\n\nMoving to Opportunity assigned participating families to its treatment and control groups by lottery, which shows that voucher allocation can indeed serve as the identifying instrument in work of this kind. Random assignment of that sort and purely observational inference are mutually exclusive: either the demonstration allocated units by lottery, or it relied entirely on observational comparisons of volunteer families with no random assignment, and exactly one of those two descriptions can hold of it. The demonstration enrolled roughly 40,000 families across twenty American cities. Its age gradients repay close attention. Children in Moving to Opportunity families who relocated after age eighteen showed the largest adult earnings gains, a pattern that stands in sharp contrast to what the interim evaluation reported about adult labor-market outcomes.\n\nThose interim results became available only after the 1994 launch of the demonstration. The interim Moving to Opportunity evaluation documented improvements in adult mental health among movers. The same interim evaluation detected no significant effects on adult employment or earnings. Adjudication between contextual and individual accounts therefore rests less on any single finding than on whether a design can credibly claim that neighborhood exposure was fixed by something other than the residents themselves.",
